orchestration with sequence \f[CR]arg\f[R]
.TP
\f[CR]\-\-stateful\f[R]
stateful operation (experimental)
.SH ARGUMENTS
\f[I]m2_\f[R] and derivative scripts must have the options specified
before command.
.PP
If the command specified can take options of its own, like
\f[CR]\-X\f[R] or \f[CR]\-\-long\-option\f[R] then the \f[CR]\-\-\f[R]
needs to be given before the command.
.PP
The \f[I]m2_\f[R] options are internally represented by shell
environment variables, eg.
\f[CR]\-s extra\f[R] will set the variable \f[CR]d_service=extra\f[R].
And similarly for the other options, the variable is named after the
option.
.PP
Some options have defaults taken from the environment, these are named
above.
.PP
Some options engender some post\-processing if possible
\f[CR]d_exec, d_options\f[R], see the MORE VARIABLES section below.
The logging variables \f[CR]\-v, \-\-log,
\-\-log\-dest and \-\-log\-deep\f[R] are discussed in the LOGGING
section below
.SH INVOCATION
The \f[CR]m2_\f[R] script itself only provides a few external commands
.PP
Derivative scripts support as many functions as are given in its
associated library script.
.SS Audit Function: \f[CR]this0\f[R]
\f[I]m2_\f[R] and the derivative scripts all support an audit function
called \f[CR]this0\f[R].
This
.SS Library Script and Definition Files
If a derivative script is named \f[CR]script0\f[R] then it should have a
library script called \f[CR]script0.sh\f[R].
scripts can also load definition files that are shell variable
definitions.
These should be named \f[CR]script0.def\f[R].
.SS Library Functions: \f[CR]d_\f[R] and \f[CR]h_\f[R] prefixes
Functions are defined within the library script, for them to accessed
from the command\-line as a command, the must be prefixed with
\f[CR]d_\f[R].
.PP
For example in \f[CR]script0.sh\f[R]
.IP
.EX
d_demo () {
  echo $*
}
.EE
.PP
Would allow \f[CR]$ script0 demo\f[R] from the shell.
A help function can be written by prefixing it with \f[CR]h\f[R], an
example might be this for the \f[CR]demo\f[R] function.
.IP
.EX
h_demo () {
  cat >&2 <<EOF

$prog ${FUNCNAME##h_} [\-c]

Prints the command\-line arguments to standard output

EOF
}
.EE
.PP
And it would be invoked like this: \f[CR]script0 \-h demo\f[R]
.PP
The order is important: the \f[CR]\-h\f[R] should appear before the
command.
The variable \f[CR]$prog\f[R] is internal to the loader\-script and will
be the loader\-script without its path.
It is derived from \f[CR]$0\f[R].
.PP
There are other function prefix conventions: \f[CR]e_\f[R] and
\f[CR]f_\f[R] are used for internal functions.
\f[CR]s_\f[R] is used for functions to be invoked as a part of stateful
operation.
.SH DEFINITIONS
.TP
loader\-script
is the \f[CR]m2_\f[R] derivative script.
In the examples above, it is \f[CR]script0\f[R].
.TP
derivative\-script
is a loader\-script but includes it library and definition files.
.TP
loader\-implementation
is the \f[CR]m2_\f[R] loader\-script itself.
It implements a derivative script by using a symbolic from the
\f[CR]m2_\f[R] to the loader script.
.TP
library\-script
a shell script file that is loaded by the loader script and has
functions defined within it prefixed with \f[CR]d_\f[R].
.TP
definitions file
a shell script file that is shell variable definitions.
.TP
script flavours
derivative scripts can have different flavour: user, host and site.
The flavour determines where the loader\-script will search for it
library and definition files.
.TP
site\-script
is a derivative script that searches the user\[aq]s own directories and
those of the host.
The \f[CR]user\f[R] directory for library and definition files is the
\f[CR]etc\f[R] directory in the \f[CR]/home/\f[R] directory of the user.
.TP
host\-script
is a derivative script that only searches the host\[aq]s directories:
\f[CR]/usr/local/etc\f[R].
.TP
user\-script
is a derivative script that only searches the directory where the script
is located.
It also searches sub\-directories named after the setting of
\f[CR]d_user\f[R] and \f[CR]d_machine\f[R].
.TP
audit\-function
this is the method \f[CR]this0\f[R] it is described below.
It is used to list the operational state of the loader\-script.
.TP
cl\-loader
is the code\-level loader.
It is a feature that allows functions from another library\-script to be
loaded by a loader\-script.
Uses the function \f[CR]d_resolve1\f[R].
.TP
name\-ref
this is a feature of BASH.
A shell variable can be passed by reference to a function.
The variable name is passed as a parameter to a function and that
function can refer to variable of the caller using
\f[CR]local \-n __ref\f[R]\[dq]$1\[dq].
Then the local variable \f[CR]__ref\f[R] will take whatever value the
function assigns to it.
That value will then be accessible on return to the caller by its
original name.
.SH SCRIPT FLAVOURS
If the loader\-implementation is on the PATH,
.PP
To create a user\-script: \f[CR]ln \-s $(which m2_) script0\f[R] To
create a site\-script: \f[CR]ln \-s $(which m2_) script0_\f[R] To create
a host\-script: \f[CR]ln \-s $(which m2_) script0__\f[R]
.PP
The underscores change the path that the loader\-implementation uses to
find the library\-script and definitions files.
.PP
There is restrictions on the name that can be given to loader\-script,
it cannot contain spaces or hyphens.
