.PP
\f[CR]support\-value\f[R] catenates the contents of the definition files
with a shell comment to show the path of the source.
.SH LOGGING
The loader\-implementation provides a full featured logging facility.
This can log to a file, or a socket.
Sockets can be TCP, TCP6, UDP and the latter can be used with IPv4
multicast.
.PP
The logging information is compliant with \f[CR]syslog\-ng\f[R].
The log format is RFC 3164, \c
.UR https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3164
.UE \c
\&.
.PP
The destination for logging is specified with the command\-line option
\f[CR]\-\-log\-dest=dest\f[R].
.PP
The logging command\-line options set these two variables that are
usually set in the loader\-implementation\[aq]s definition files.
These settings send to multicast and the log level of 2 will make
logging propagate.
.IP
.EX
_M_GLOBAL_LOG_DST=mcast://239.255.0.2:17777
_M_GLOBAL_LOG_LVL=2
.EE
.PP
These settings are loaded into the loader\-script, so it is not enough
to disable logging in the environment.
You have to unset these variables in the files.
Use the audit\-function to see if they are enabled
.IP
.EX
script0 this0 support\-value | grep \-E \[aq]\[ha]#|_LOG_)
.EE
.PP
To disable logging use a local definition file and set
\f[CR]_M_GLOBAL_LOG_LVL=0\f[R]
.IP
.EX
$ cat script0.def
_M_GLOBAL_LOG_DST=$PWD/$prog.log
_M_GLOBAL_LOG_LVL=0
.EE
.PP
To enable logging to standard error, set \f[CR]_M_GLOBAL_LOG_LVL=1\f[R]
.PP
To enable logging to the file, set \f[CR]_M_GLOBAL_LOG_LVL=2\f[R].
.PP
Lines to be sent to the logger use a dedicated file descriptor, which is
\f[CR]7\f[R]
.PP
Here is a test function to be used for those different configurations.
.IP
.EX
d_log0 () {
  echo $FUNCNAME standard output >& 1
  echo $FUNCNAME standard error >& 2
  echo $FUNCNAME logger >& 7
}
.EE
.PP
It is also possible to specify the destination for logging with the
command\-line option: \f[CR]\-\-log\-dest=t.log\f[R].
This also sets the \f[CR]_M_GLOBAL_LOG_LVL\f[R] value.
.SS Network Logging and \f[CR]syslog\-ng\f[R]
To send logging messages to a network destination, you can use
\f[CR]socat\f[R] on a TCP or TCP6 socket.
When used in this way, the log messages will contain the RFC 3164
control information.
This method does not need root privileges.
.PP
When used with \f[CR]syslog\-ng\f[R], it is safer to run
\f[CR]syslog\-ng\f[R] from a user account and use \f[CR]setcap\f[R] to
allow unprivileged users to run it.
.IP
.EX
/usr/bin/sudo /usr/sbin/setcap cap_net_bind_service=ep /usr/sbin/syslog\-ng
.EE
.PP
In a user systemd unit, this is a useful invocation
.IP
.EX
ExecStart=/usr/sbin/syslog\-ng ${X_ARGS} \[rs]
    \-\-foreground \[rs]
    \-\-cfgfile=%E/syslog\-trace/syslog\-trace.conf \[rs]
    \-\-pidfile=%t/syslog\-trace.pid \[rs]
    \-\-control=%t/syslog\-trace.ctl \[rs]
    \-R %S/syslog\-trace.persist
.EE
.PP
And this is a simple configuration for \f[CR]syslog\-ng\f[R].
You will need to define the domain names \f[CR]local0.6site0\f[R] and
\f[CR]local0.site0\f[R] or not use them at all and remove them from the
configuration.
.PP
You will need to change the UID of 1000 to your own.
.PP
The multicast network is mcast://239.255.0.2:17777
.IP
.EX
\[at]version: 4.4
# \[at]include \[dq]scl.conf\[dq]

# simple processing for the m2_ utility:
# on the development LAN mcast://239.255.0.2:17777

options {
    time\-reap(30);
    mark\-freq(0);
    keep\-hostname(yes);
    # Since we are running in user space, disable privileged controls
    create\-dirs(yes);

    use\-fqdn(yes);
};

# IPv6 unicast
source s_tu6network {
    network(
        ip(local0.6site0)
        transport(\[dq]tcp\[dq])
        port(17777)
        ip\-protocol(6)
    );
};

# IPv4 multicast
source s_um4network {
  network(
          ip(\[dq]239.255.0.2\[dq])
          transport(\[dq]udp\[dq])
          port(17777)
          ip\-protocol(4)
          );
};

# Example Destination: Standard user\-journal integration or a clean text terminal log
destination d_file {
    file(\[dq]\[ga]XDG_CACHE_HOME\[ga]/trace.log\[dq]);
};

destination d_xconsole_remote {
  pipe(\[dq]/run/user/1000/xconsole\[dq]);
};

destination d_xconsole_local {
  pipe(\[dq]/run/user/1000/xconsole1\[dq]);
};

log {
    source(s_um4network);
    destination(d_xconsole);
    destination(d_file);
};
.EE
.PP
You will need to add some pipes for \f[CR]d_xconsole\f[R] and
\f[CR]d_xconsole1\f[R].
This can be done by systemd.
.IP
.EX
$ cat \[ti]/.config/user\-tmpfiles.d/*
# Type  Path               Mode  UID  GID  Age  Argument
p       %t/xconsole        0600  \-    \-    \-    \-
p       %t/xconsole1       0600  \-    \-    \-    \-
.EE
.PP
And then a user systemd unit can be implemented to display the pipe.
.IP
.EX
ExecStart=/usr/bin/xterm \-class XVTerm \-name %i \-title %N \-e \[aq]cat < \[dq]$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/%i\[dq]\[aq]
.EE
.PP
Where \f[CR]%i=xconsole\f[R] and there is some minor specialization of
the xterm class.
.PP
When it is all working, you will see something like this in the
\f[CR]XVTerm\f[R] instance.
.IP
.EX
2026\-10\-07T20:25:30.185 maria.6site0 local0.debug script0[939422]: Tracing via mcast 239.255.0.2 17777
<135>2026\-10\-07T20:25:30.199 maria.6site0 script0[939422]: # d_resolve1: [d_ls] successfully loaded from [/usr/local/etc/m_loadr/m_.sh]
<135>2026\-10\-07T20:25:30.203 maria.6site0 script0[939422]: # d_resolve1: [f_orchestrate] successfully loaded from [/usr/local/etc/m_loadr/m2_.sh]
<135>2026\-10\-07T20:25:30.208 maria.6site0 script0[939422]: # d_resolve1: [e_orchestrate] successfully loaded from [/usr/local/etc/m_loadr/m2_.sh]
2026\-10\-07T20:25:30.218 maria.6site0 local0.debug script0[939422]: 2026\-10\-07T20:25:30.211 [script0:d_log0] (939422) Start: weaves\[at]maria:/misc/sistemoj/prj\-public/src/m2_/demo
2026\-10\-07T20:25:30.221 maria.6site0 local0.debug script0[939422]: d_log0 logger
2026\-10\-07T20:25:30.223 maria.6site0 local0.debug script0[939422]: 2026\-10\-07T20:25:30.219 [script0:d_log0] (939422) Exit: 0
.EE
.SS Special Logging Levels
By default, the logging is to the facility \f[CR]local0\f[R] and at
\f[CR]debug\f[R] level.
.PP
If you need to change this, there is a function \f[CR]d_alert\f[R]
.IP
.EX
d_alert 2 the message to display
.EE
.PP
The number \[dq]2\[dq] will raise at \[dq]critical\[dq].
.PP
Another way to raise a syslog message at a different level is to use
this format
.IP
.EX
printf \[dq]<138> [%s] level\[rs]n\[dq] \[dq]$FUNCNAME:$e_cmd\[dq] >&7
.EE
.PP
This send a logging line to the logging file descriptor.
The number 138 can be calculated in
.PP
\f[CR]PRI = (Facility × 8) + Severity\f[R]
.PP
