.SH USER LOADER SCRIPTS \- USER AND MACHINE EXTENSIONS
User\-loader scripts are those that load their script libraries and
definitions from the same directory that the loader\-script is in.
.PP
This provides an extra feature.
If the \f[CR]\-m\f[R] option is given on the command\-line it will load
additional script libraries from the sub\-directory named by the
\f[I]machine\f[R] parameter to \f[CR]\-m machine\f[R] by default it is
the current host.
.PP
On a machine called \f[CR]kaya\f[R], the audit\-function reports this
library file and definitions:
.IP
.EX
$ script0 this0 support
/misc/sistemoj/prj\-public/src/m2_/demo/script0.sh
weaves:devel\[at]kaya:.../src/m2_/demo
$ script0 this0 support\-def
/srv/nfs4/sistemoj/site/etc/m2_.def
/srv/nfs4/sistemoj/site/share/site/cluster/m2_.def
/srv/nfs4/sistemoj/site/share/site/kaya/m2_.def
/misc/sistemoj/prj\-public/src/m2_/demo/script0.def
.EE
.PP
The script is being run from the directory:
\f[CR]/misc/sistemoj/prj\-public/src/m2_/demo\f[R] and it finds the
\f[CR]script0.sh\f[R] library file.
.PP
For the definitions, it finds the loader\-implementation\[aq]s
definition files; m2_.def and finally the script0.def file.
.PP
They are loaded in that order and that feature was used to \f[B]not\f[R]
use the default log destination in the earlier discussion of logging.
.PP
On a machine called \f[CR]maria\f[R], the same commands use an existing
sub\-directory for \f[I]maria\f[R] to load extra scripts and
definitions.
.IP
.EX
$ script0 this0 support
/misc/sistemoj/prj\-public/src/m2_/demo/script0.sh
/misc/sistemoj/prj\-public/src/m2_/demo/maria/script0.sh
$ script0 this0 support\-def
/srv/nfs4/sistemoj/site/etc/m2_.def
/srv/nfs4/sistemoj/site/share/site/cluster/m2_.def
/misc/sistemoj/prj\-public/src/m2_/demo/script0.def
/misc/sistemoj/prj\-public/src/m2_/demo/maria/script0.def
.EE
.PP
The \f[I]machine\f[R] can be given explicitly with \f[CR]\-m maria\f[R]
when run on the machine kaya.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-m maria\f[R] sets the variable \f[CR]d_machine\f[R] this can
also be set in the shell environment when the script\-loader is run.
.PP
The \f[I]machine\f[R] variant need not of course be a machine.
It could be any variant you might want to load.
For example, \f[CR]\-m strongswan\f[R] would search a sub\-directory
strongswan/ for a file script0.sh and load that.
This script\-library might contain additional code to manage StrongSWAN
VPN connections.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-u user\f[R] command\-line option can be used in the same way
as the \f[CR]\-m
machine\f[R] option.
It will load after the \f[I]machine\f[R] script\-library.
.PP
You may also have noticed other differences between the kaya and maria
audit\-function reports for support\-def.
kaya also loads
\f[CR]/srv/nfs4/sistemoj/site/share/site/kaya/m2_.def\f[R].
There is a feature of the audit\-function to see what the load path is:
\f[CR]script0 this0 mloader\f[R].
.PP
The site\-form loaders can also load machine and user specific
definition files.
You can see the behaviour here:
.IP
.EX
$ script0 this0 vpaths
declare \-a SYS_VPATH=([0]=\[dq]/usr/local/etc\[dq] [1]=\[dq]/usr/local/share/site/cluster\[dq])
declare \-a USER_VPATH=([0]=\[dq]/usr/local/etc\[dq] [1]=\[dq]/usr/local/share/site/cluster\[dq] [2]=\[dq]/home/weaves/etc\[dq] [3]=\[dq]/home/weaves/share/site/maria\[dq])
declare \-a APP_VPATH=([0]=\[dq]/misc/sistemoj/prj\-public/src/m2_/demo\[dq] [1]=\[dq]/misc/sistemoj/prj\-public/src/m2_/demo/maria\[dq])
declare \-a CLLR_VPATH=([0]=\[dq]/srv/nfs4/sistemoj/site/etc\[dq] [1]=\[dq]/srv/nfs4/sistemoj/site/share/site/cluster\[dq])
declare \-\- THIS_VPATH_NAME=\[dq]APP_VPATH\[dq]
.EE
.PP
script0 has THIS~VPATHNAME~ designating it APP~VPATH~ which is
name\-form.
USER~VPATH~ is host\-form with one trailing \f[CR]_\f[R] or site\-form.
.PP
And SYS~VPATH~ is for script\-loaders which are host\-form and have two
underscores \f[CR]__\f[R].
.PP
This is another loader\-script.
Where the root user has a directory \f[CR]\[ti]/share/site/maria\f[R]
which has a toor__.def in it.
The vpaths are respectively: \f[CR]__\f[R], one \f[CR]_\f[R] and no
.IP
.EX
maria# toor__ this0 vpaths
declare \-a SYS_VPATH=([0]=\[dq]/usr/local/etc\[dq] [1]=\[dq]/usr/local/share/site/cluster\[dq])
declare \-a USER_VPATH=([0]=\[dq]/usr/local/etc\[dq] [1]=\[dq]/usr/local/share/site/cluster\[dq] [2]=\[dq]/root/share/site/maria\[dq])
declare \-a APP_VPATH=([0]=\[dq]/usr/local/sbin\[dq])
declare \-a CLLR_VPATH=([0]=\[dq]/srv/nfs4/sistemoj/site/etc\[dq] [1]=\[dq]/srv/nfs4/sistemoj/site/share/site/cluster\[dq])
declare \-\- THIS_VPATH_NAME=\[dq]SYS_VPATH\[dq]
.EE
.PP
The CLLR~VPATH~ is used by the cl\-loader.
The Code\-Level Loader.
.SH CODE\-LEVEL LOADING
Another feature of a loader\-script is the cl\-loader (code\-level
loader).
This makes it possible to load functions from other library\-script.
It can be invoked at code\-level using the loader\-implementation
function \f[CR]d_resolve1\f[R].
It is also invoked on the initial load of a loader\-script.
To understand how it could work, this illustrates it not working in the
initial load.
.PP
Using the demonstration loader\-script, try and load a function that
doesn\[aq]t exist because we have misspelled the name.
Here stating \f[CR]tihs0\f[R] and not \f[CR]this0\f[R]
.IP
.EX
$ script0 tihs0 support

!!! ERROR REPORT !!!
[d_resolve1] (Error 4): Library file [script0.sh] not found in /usr/local/etc/m_loadr.
!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!
.EE
.PP
The error tells us that a library file could not be found in the
directory \f[CR]/usr/local/etc/m_loadr\f[R].
.PP
That directory is specified by the environment variable
\f[CR]_M__LOADR_\f[R], which can be seen with
\f[CR]script0 this0 support\-value\f[R]
.IP
.EX
# /srv/nfs4/sistemoj/site/etc/m2_.def
_M__LOADR_=/usr/local/etc/m_loadr
.EE
.PP
On this system, the \f[CR]/usr/local/etc/m_loadr\f[R] directory contains
two files:
.IP
.EX
$ ls /usr/local/etc/m_loadr
m2_.sh  m_.sh
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]m_\f[R] is the library script for library scripts.
It contains nearly a hundred functions that can be used.
.PP
If I try to find the badly named function in that:
.IP
.EX
$ m_ tihs0 support

!!! ERROR REPORT !!!
[d_resolve1] (Error 8): Extraction failed. Function [d_tihs0] not in [m_.sh].
!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]d_resolve1\f[R] function now reports a different error, it
failed to find the function in the library\-script in
\f[CR]/usr/local/etc/m_loadr/m_.sh\f[R]
.PP
The demonstration \f[CR]script0.sh\f[R] library\-script contains this
call \f[CR]d_resolve1 d_ls m_\f[R].
This loads the function \f[CR]d_ls\f[R] from the library\-script
\f[CR]m_.sh\f[R] in the \f[I]m~loadr~\f[R] directory.
.PP
If we pre\-load \f[CR]d_ls\f[R] and have a function in our user\-form
script script0.sh that uses it:
.IP
.EX

# the pre\-load can be made when the library\-script is loaded
# d_resolve1 d_ls m_

d_ls1 () {
  local e_cmd=$1
  shift

  # or the pre\-load can be made when the function is used.
  d_resolve1 d_ls m_

  case $e_cmd in
    inode\-idx)
      find ${d_dir:\-$PWD} \-type f \-printf \[dq]%i %p\[rs]n\[dq]
      ;;

    undot) # calling d_ls directly
      d_ls undot $*
      ;;
    *) # using d_ls as a fall\-through.
      d_ls $e_cmd $*
      ;;
  esac
}
.EE
.PP
So the function \f[CR]d_ls1\f[R] not supports \f[CR]m_ ls undot\f[R] by
a named pass\-through and supports \f[CR]mergerfs\f[R] by the unnamed
fall\-through.
.IP
.EX
$ find . \-type f | script0 ls1 undot
script0.sh
maria/script0.sh
maria/script0.def
demo.log
script0.def
work.log
script0.err
make.log
numbers.lst
bill/script0.sh
t.log
$ script0 ls1 mergerfs /media/tv\-0/Youth\[rs] \[rs](2026\[rs])\[rs] \[rs][tvdbid\-470000\[rs]]/
getfattr: Removing leading \[aq]/\[aq] from absolute path names
# file: media/tv\-0/Youth (2026) [tvdbid\-470000]/
user.mergerfs.allpaths=\[dq]/a/l/RA\-media/share/tv0/Youth (2026) [tvdbid\-470000]\[rs]000/a/l/SI\-media/share/tv0/Youth (2026) [tvdbid\-470000]\[rs]000/a/l/SM\-media/share/tv0/Youth (2026) [tvdbid\-470000]\[dq]
.EE
.PP
In this way, loader\-scripts need not include a whole library\-script
using the BASH \f[CR]source\f[R] directive.
It can selectively include just one function.
.PP
The pre\-load function \f[CR]d_resolve1\f[R] takes two parameters: the
function to load and the filename (not the path) of the library\-script
in the \f[CR]/usr/local/etc/m_loadr/m_.sh\f[R].
.SH ORCHESTRATE \- SEQUENCED OPERATIONS
The demo loader\-script also illustrates the \f[CR]\-\-orchestrate\f[R]
command\-line option.
This allows a sequence of operations to be executed.
It is most often used to generate files.
.IP
.EX
d_resolve1 f_orchestrate m2_
d_resolve1 e_orchestrate m2_

e_fragments0=\[dq]\[dq]

d_fragments0 () {
  local cmd=\[dq]${1:\-}\[dq]

  case $cmd in
  header0)
    e_fragments0=\[dq]${cmd}\-$RANDOM\[dq]
    ;;
  body0)
    e_fragments0=\[dq] $e_fragments0 ${cmd}\-$RANDOM\[dq]
    ;;
  footer0)
    e_fragments0=\[dq] $e_fragments0 ${cmd}\-$RANDOM\[dq]
    ;;
  print0)
    echo $e_fragments0
    ;;
  esac

}

d_example1 () {
  e_orchestrate d_fragments0
}
.EE
.PP
The two functions \f[CR]e_orchestrate\f[R] and \f[CR]f_orchestrate\f[R]
are pre\-loaded.
A generator function: \f[CR]d_fragments0\f[R] has been implemented
supporting sub\-commands.
The \f[CR]\-\-orchestrate=arg\f[R] is specified as a sequence of
sub\-commands separated by a comma, \f[CR],\f[R].
.PP
The co\-ordinating function \f[CR]d_example1\f[R] is used to invoke
\f[CR]e_orchestrate\f[R] using \f[CR]d_fragments0\f[R].
The \f[CR]\-\-orchestrate=<sequence>\f[R] is then processed by the
\f[CR]d_example1\f[R] call to \f[CR]e_orchestrate\f[R].
.IP
.EX
$ script0 \-\-orchestrate=header0,body0,footer0,print0 example1
header0\-30431 body0\-17640 footer0\-1844
.EE
.PP
And other sequences can be given:
.IP
.EX
$ script0 \-\-orchestrate=header0,body0,body0,body0,footer0,print0 example1
header0\-2288 body0\-15399 body0\-7306 body0\-6741 footer0\-6311
.EE
.PP
Notice that because all the function calls that \f[CR]e_orchestrate\f[R]
are within the same process, the shell variable used to collate the
result, \f[CR]e_fragments0\f[R] can accumulate.
.PP
This should work across sub\-shells: so a new sub\-command added to
\f[CR]d_fragments0\f[R]
.IP
.EX
body1)
  e_fragments0=$(echo $e_fragments0 ${cmd}\-$RANDOM)\[dq]
  ;;
.EE
.PP
would do the same as the \f[CR]body0\f[R] sub\-command.
.PP
If the \f[CR]e_fragments0\f[R] is exported, it can be operated upon
directly by the sub\-shell.
.PP
About naming of functions: \f[CR]d_fragments0\f[R] has been given the
\f[CR]d_\f[R] prefix so that one can test the separate sub\-commands
directly from the command\-line: \f[CR]script0 fragments0 body0\f[R] for
example.
.PP
The function could have any name when it is called by
\f[CR]e_orchestrate\f[R].
.SH ADDING SHELL VARIABLES \- EVLR
There is a function provided by the loader\-implementation
\f[CR]d_evlr\f[R] which will set shell variables.
The string to process is of the form \f[CR]a=b,c=d\f[R].
Given this string, \f[CR]d_evlr\f[R] will set the shell a and b as shell
variables with the values \[dq]b\[dq] and \[dq]d\[dq].
.PP
This use of \f[CR]d_evlr\f[R] has been made available on the
command\-line options using two options: \f[CR]\-o arg\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-e arg\f[R].
.PP
In the demonstration script, \f[CR]d_xoptions\f[R] illustrates this:
.IP
.EX
$ script0 \-othis=that xoptions
this=that

this : \[dq]that\[dq]
.EE
.PP
The option \f[CR]\-e arg\f[R] does exactly the same, but is expected to
be used for programs.
\f[CR]d_xoptions\f[R] also demonstrates the shell variable are set by
the \f[CR]\-e arg\f[R]
.IP
.EX
$ script0 \-ethis=that \-othese=those xoptions
these=those
this=that
this : \[dq]that\[dq]
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]\-e arg\f[R] is usually used to select a function to be used
within a function.
The function \f[CR]d_exec0\f[R] illustrates this.
.IP
.EX
$ script0 exec0
cat
1
2
3
4
5
$ script0 \-e \[aq]wc \-l\[aq] exec0
wc \-l
5
.EE
.PP
The default the \f[CR]\-e arg\f[R] is cat.
In the second invocation, \f[CR]wc \-l\f[R] is passed.
.SH UTILITY METHODS \- TEMP FILES, STACKS AND OTHERS
The loader\-implementation provides a number of utility methods.
Use the audit\-function like this:
.IP
.EX
$ m2_ \-a this0 loaded
d_ d_alert d_alert_log d_evlr d_help d_recurse0 d_recurse1 d_recurse2 d_recurse3
d_recurse4 d_resolve1 d_settings0 d_settings1 d_this0 e__a e_assert0 e_cstack0
e_env e_env_export e_env_init e_env_show e_err_fatal0 e_err_report0 e_fs_source0
e_fsstk_reset e_is_logging e_mod_loader e_mod_lock e_nodo e_path_parse
e_ptr_resolve e_state_dump e_state_load e_state_save e_stk_empty0 e_stk_peek0
e_stk_pop0 e_stk_push0 e_stk_show0 e_tpush e_tpush1 e_yorn
.EE
.SS Temporary Files \- \f[CR]e_tpush1\f[R]
Temporary files can be managed by the loader\-script using the
loader\-implementation method \f[CR]e_tpush1\f[R]
.PP
To get a temporary file, create a local variable to contain the path
generated for a temporary file and pass that variable name to the
function \f[CR]e_tpush1\f[R].
The variable name is used as a local name\-ref.
.IP
.EX
local tfile
e_tpush1 \[dq]tfile\[dq]
.EE
.PP
The function is called \f[CR]e_tpush1\f[R] because it uses a stack to
hold the temporary file names.
These are then deleted when the loader\-script exits.
.SS Stacks \- \f[CR]e_stk_\f[R]
The loader\-script implements a stack for shell variables.
.PP
The functions are: e~stkempty0~ e~stkpeek0~ e~stkpop0~ e~stkpush0~
e~stkshow0~.
.PP
These require the use of a \f[B]sentinel\f[R] string value which has to
be passed to every stack function.
(It hasn\[aq]t been possible to invent a global null value for all
stacks.)
Here is some sample code that illustrates setting up and filling a stack
for a Finite State Machine.
.SS Paths
\f[CR]e_path_parse\f[R] is a function that parses a path and returns it
as an array
.PP
Invoked like this
\f[CR]local \-a path0; e_path_parse /tmp/t.out \[dq]path0\[dq]\f[R].
It only uses BASH variable substitutions.
.PP
This is the array for a path (left column) and a URI (right column)
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l l.
T{
0
T}@T{
Directory
T}@T{
Hostname/IP
T}
T{
1
T}@T{
Basename
T}@T{
Full address (host port)
T}
T{
2
T}@T{
Extension
T}@T{
Scheme (tcp socket http)
T}
T{
3
T}@T{
Filebase
T}@T{
Base path
T}
T{
4
T}@T{
Depth
T}@T{
Depth of base path
T}
T{
5
T}@T{
\-
T}@T{
Port
T}
.TE
.PP
It must contain \f[CR]://\f[R] to be considered as a URI.
Here are some examples: First a path, and then some URIs.
.IP
.EX
$ script0 path0 $PWD/script0.sh
declare \-a path0=([0]=\[dq]/misc/sistemoj/prj\-public/src/m2_/demo\[dq] [1]=\[dq]script0.sh\[dq] [2]=\[dq]sh\[dq] [3]=\[dq]script0\[dq] [4]=\[dq]6\[dq] [5]=\[dq]\[dq])
$ script0 path0 http://polly.6site0:9091/transmission
declare \-a path0=([0]=\[dq]polly.6site0\[dq] [1]=\[dq]polly.6site0:9091/transmission\[dq] [2]=\[dq]http\[dq] [3]=\[dq]/transmission\[dq] [4]=\[dq]13\[dq] [5]=\[dq]9091\[dq])
$ script0 path0 mcast://238.255.0.2:17777
declare \-a path0=([0]=\[dq]238.255.0.2\[dq] [1]=\[dq]238.255.0.2:17777\[dq] [2]=\[dq]mcast\[dq] [3]=\[dq]\[dq] [4]=\[dq]0\[dq] [5]=\[dq]17777\[dq])
$ script0 path0 file://$PWD/script0.sh
declare \-a path0=([0]=\[dq]\[dq] [1]=\[dq]/misc/sistemoj/prj\-public/src/m2_/demo/script0.sh\[dq] [2]=\[dq]file\[dq] [3]=\[dq]/misc/sistemoj/prj\-public/src/m2_/demo/script0.sh\[dq] [4]=\[dq]49\[dq] [5]=\[dq]\[dq])
.EE
.SH CODE SAFETY ERROR TRAPS AND TRACING
The loader\-implementation implements the \f[B]no unset variable\f[R]
requirement for BASH, that is \f[CR]set \-o nounset\f[R] or simply
\f[CR]set \-u\f[R].
This means that an unset variable cannot be used.
.IP
.EX
$ script0 path0 
/misc/sistemoj/prj\-public/src/m2_/demo/script0.sh: line 357: 1: unbound variable

!!! ERROR REPORT !!!
[script0] Unknown failure in d_path0 (Status: \-1)
!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!
.EE
.PP
The error message above also illustrates that a line number is given
with an error.
This is an addition to BASH and can help track down mistakes in large
scripts.
.PP
The code that caused the error is this:
.IP
.EX
d_path0 () {
  local \-a path0=()
  e_path_parse \[dq]${1}\[dq] path0
  declare \-p path0
}
.EE
.PP
It\[aq]s fairly simple assign an empty string as default with:
\f[CR]\[dq]${1:\-}\[dq]\f[R], but this might pass the problem onto the
function.
If you want to handle the error and not return the \f[CR]255\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-1\f[R] error level, then
\f[CR]test \-n \[dq]${1:\-}\[dq] || return 1\f[R].
You can also set the error message to be used:
\f[CR]test \-n \[dq]${1:\-}\[dq] || { d_ERRMSG\f[R]\[dq]a path or URI is
required\[dq]; return 1; }=
.PP
The error handling is dealt with my BASH traps.
Because of that, it\[aq]s recommended that you exit functions using
\f[CR]return\f[R] rather than \f[CR]exit\f[R].
When a function returns, it goes back to the caller which has some
additional processing it can perform.
.PP
To trace the execution of the library\-script, use the \f[CR]\-x\f[R]
flag.
This avoids tracing the start\-up and loading code of the
loader\-implementation.
You will see trace from the loader\-implementation\[aq]s library\-script
if you use any of the utility methods.
.PP
If you need to develop the loader\-implementation, you can use the
system trace variable: \f[CR]: ${_M__TRACE_:\f[R]\[dq]\-\[dq]}= is its
default value, ie.
\f[CR]\-\f[R] do nothing.
.PP
Values can be assigned: \f[CR]d, n, p\f[R] are the values, several can
be set together.
.TP
d
debug, ie.
trace the loader\-implementation code and the library\-script.
.TP
n
nodo, ie.
set the shell variable \f[CR]nodo\f[R] to the value \f[CR]echo\f[R],
usually it is an empty string.
.TP
p
no\-nounset, do not activate the \f[CR]set \-o unset\f[R] feature, so
that unset variables can be used.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The environment variables that are specific to loader\-implementation
are these:
\f[CR]_M__LOADR_ _M_FIFO_OWNED _M_GLOBAL_LOG_DST _M_GLOBAL_LOG_LVL _M_LOG_MODE _M_P_BASE _M_P_DEPTH _M_P_DIR _M_P_EXT _M_P_FILE _M__DESC_ _M__LOADR_ _M__LOG_ _M__TRACE_ _M__VARS_ _M__VER_\f[R]
.PP
Any of the command\-line options and some specialized internals can be
viewed with the audit\-function: \f[CR]script0 this0 settings\f[R]
.PP
\f[CR]d_ERRMSG d_aflag d_args d_count d_dir d_exec d_file d_group d_helpflag d_implementation d_interface d_log d_machine d_options d_orchestrate d_output d_password d_port d_ref d_service d_stateful d_user verbose\f[R]
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION ENVIRONMENT
The environment is as the calling shell.
The environment has only \f[CR]set \-o
nounset\f[R].
Many of the loader\-implementation utilities have been implemented, or
have alternative implementations, that use local name\-refs to pass
parameters and return values via these.
.PP
These features are only available in later versions of BASH, in
particular, version 5.2.
.PP
The loader\-implementation may have made assumptions about shared
environment and file\-descriptors.
This will only be an issue with different forms of function invocations,
when the function is defined in the same library\-script, either
directly stated or when loaded by \f[CR]d_resolve1\f[R] or equivalently
by means of BASH \f[CR]source file\f[R].
.PP
These invocations have been improved in BASH 5.2 to take place in the
same process space and will share its environment and file descriptors.
In these examples, the \f[CR]d_function\f[R] could be any shell
function.
.TP
\f[CR]$(d_function)\f[R]
usually in this way \f[CR]local val=$(d_function)\f[R].
.TP
\f[CR]<(d_function)\f[R]
usually used for piping input:
\f[CR]while read i; do echo $i; done < <(d_function)\f[R]
.TP
\f[CR]<<< $(d_function)\f[R]
usually used like this:
\f[CR]while read i; do echo $i; done < <<< $(d_function)\f[R]
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status is usually the return value of the last executed
command; this can be overridden by explicitly stating
\f[CR]return 0\f[R] or some other value.
You may choose to return \f[CR]\-1\f[R], this will trigger the
\f[CR]ERR\f[R] and report the line number.
.PP
If a syntax error or some other BASH parsing error (unbalanced
delimiters, for example), then the exit value will be \f[CR]\-1\f[R].
.PP
Rarely, other errors will be generated, \f[CR]pipefail\f[R] may arise
within the logging component.
.SH BUG REPORTS
If you do find a bug or some erroneous or inconsistent behaviour feel
free to report it via GitHub.
.SH BUGS
Currently, although the \f[CR]\-\-stateful\f[R] sub\-system does work,
but it is experimental and is only demonstrated in the GitHub source.
.PP
The \f[CR]\-\-log\f[R] command\-line option does not work correctly.
Use \f[CR]\-\-log\-dest=file\f[R] for a simple logging to file with the
\f[CR]>& 7\f[R] file\-descriptor.
